<br>
Hi Justin,<br>
<br>
Emma Howrilla in Durham Planning referred me to you (Infrastructure<br>
Review). I'm doing due-diligence on a possible purchase of 1621<br>
Clermont Rd, Durham 27713 (PIN 0728808242 / REID 153775, ~9.4 ac) and<br>
need to understand the access requirement before going further.<br>
<br>
Situation: the buildable ground is the upland corner fronting Clermont<br>
Rd. The Clermont right-of-way in front of the parcel is unmaintained -the<b=
r>
the pavement dead-ends roughly 258 ft east of Euclid Rd. Sewer is<br>
available via an on-site easement; water would need to be extended.<br>
<br>
Questions:<br>
1. For a single dwelling - or a duplex - on that frontage, would the<br>
end of Clermont have to be built out to a public-street standard, or<br>
can a private driveway off the existing pavement satisfy access? <span styl=
e=3D"color: rgb(200, 38, 19);">
Yes, a City street would need to be built along with extended the City wate=
r main under the new street.</span><br>
2. If a build-out is required, what standard applies (pavement width,<br>
length to the site, turnaround/cul-de-sac, sidewalk)? That lets me<br>
scope cost with a civil engineer. <span style=3D"color: rgb(200, 38, 19);">=
Extend the same width as the existing street along curb and gutter and side=
walk. If the street extension from the intersection is more than 300' a per=
manent cul-de-sac would be required.</span><br>
3. Does the answer change if I create one or two lots via a minor<br>
subdivision vs. building on the existing single parcel? <span style=3D"colo=
r: rgb(200, 38, 19);">
No, the answer would be the same.</span><br>
<br>
I've attached a Durham GIS utility/ROW map and a parcel map showing<br>
where the pavement ends. Happy to send anything else useful.<br>
<br>
Thank you,<br>
Kalin Ivanov</div>
